  "notes": "combined_matrix_camera_to_linear_prophoto.match_prototype is what this Python prototype's render_stage()/pipeline.py actually applies to LibRaw/dcraw's as-shot-white-balanced camera RGB (cam_linear_wb): it is ForwardMatrix(whiteXY) folded with XYZ(D50)->ProPhoto, with NO additional per-channel normalization. The real DNG SDK (dng_color_spec::SetWhiteXY) instead recomputes fCameraWhite = colorMatrix(whiteXY) @ XYZ(whiteXY) (max-entry-normalized, pinned to [0.001, 1.0]) and folds diag(1/fCameraWhite) into the transform applied to PRE-white-balance camera values. IMPORTANT: neutral_ratio_rgb (== [1/WBRedLevel, 1/WBGreenLevel, 1/WBBlueLevel]) is on an arbitrary absolute scale, NOT the DNG-spec AsShotNeutral scale -- the as-shot WB gain dcraw's -w actually applies is level[c]/level[green] (green-normalized; WBGreenLevel is a fixed 256 across all three photos here while Red/Blue vary with CCT), i.e. cam_linear_wb == raw * diag(neutral_g1_normalized_rgb) elementwise, so the SDK-faithful transform re-expressed for cam_linear_wb input is combined_matrix_camera_to_linear_prophoto.sdk_camera_white == match_prototype @ diag(neutral_g1_normalized_rgb / camera_white_sdk) -- using raw neutral_ratio_rgb here instead would introduce a spurious ~WBGreenLevel-times uniform scale error, not a small correction. neutral_g1_normalized_rgb and camera_white_sdk are close but not identical (fCameraWhite is re-derived from the converged white xy through the CCT-interpolated ColorMatrix, not reused directly from AsShotNeutral), so the two combined matrices differ by up to max_abs_diff_combined_matrix per photo -- small but real. Swift's ColorSpec.swift implements both as `.matchPrototype` (must equal match_prototype here) and `.sdkCameraWhite` (must equal sdk_camera_white here, using neutral_g1_normalized_rgb; this is the default used by AdobeColorMath)."
